Ask the H.264 parser for reference lists on a slice-based decoder.

The V4L2 stateless delegate never said it needed reference lists, so the
parser left them empty. VA-API does ask. On the first P-slice the header
names one reference, the empty list cannot supply it, and Chromium falls
back to software before the driver sees the slice. An I-slice has no list,
which is why that slice's controls were already accepted.

Slice-based devices now ask for the lists. Spare slots at the start of a
GOP, where the header asks for more entries than pictures exist, repeat the
last real reference. The driver checks every active index, and the bitstream
does not address the spare ones. A frame-based device still gets the old
answer, so ChromeOS hardware is unchanged. This is the stateless uAPI, not
an Apple-only change.

